🔢 3. Dimensional Cardinality (Metric Cardinality)

🔹 Definition

Dimensional Cardinality is the count of unique metric time series (MTS) produced by the combination of a metric name and its dimensions (labels / attributes).

Each unique tuple of
metric name + attribute values = 1 time series.
In Kubernetes this may include cluster, namespace, pod, container, node, region, etc.

Excessive dimensions (especially dynamic ones like pod_uid) create a cardinality explosion that increases cost, ingestion volume, and query latency.

⸻

🔹 Example 1 – High Cardinality (Non-Optimized)

Metric name: k8s.container.cpu.usageCores
Attributes:
  cluster = "prod-cluster"
  namespace = "checkout"
  pod = "checkout-api-56f7d5b99c-2jzdp"
  container_id = "docker://8af4e"

Every new container_id creates a new MTS.
A 100-pod deployment may generate hundreds of unique series for a single metric.

⸻

🔹 Example 2 – Optimized Cardinality

Metric name: k8s.container.cpu.usageCores
Attributes:
  cluster = "prod-cluster"
  namespace = "checkout"
  deployment = "checkout-api"

Stable dimensions reduce series dramatically (from thousands → tens).

⸻

🔹 Example 3 – Exploding Dimension: Pod UID

If OpenTelemetry is configured to include pod_uid, each pod restart creates a new time series:

k8s.container.cpu.usageCores{pod_uid="cfe1234"} 
k8s.container.cpu.usageCores{pod_uid="cfe5678"} 
...

→ This doubles or triples cardinality after each rolling deployment.

Fix: Drop pod_uid attribute using the Transform Processor.

processors:
  transform:
    metric_statements:
      - context: metric
        statements:
          - delete_key(attributes, "pod_uid")


⸻

🔹 Example 4 – Namespace Aggregation to Reduce Cardinality

Group metrics by namespace instead of individual pods:

processors:
  groupbyattrs:
    keys: [ "cluster", "namespace", "deployment" ]

Resulting metric:

k8s.container.cpu.usageCores{cluster="prod",namespace="checkout",deployment="checkout-api"}

→ All pods of checkout-api share one series per namespace.

⸻

🔹 Example 5 – Combine Dynamic Labels into Static Sets

Transform labels with stable identifiers:

processors:
  transform:
    metric_statements:
      - context: metric
        statements:
          - set(attributes["workload"], attributes["k8s.deployment.name"])
          - delete_key(attributes, "pod_name")
          - delete_key(attributes, "container_id")


⸻

🔹 NRQL Example – Estimate Cardinality

FROM Metric 
SELECT count(unique(metricTimesliceName)) 
WHERE instrumentation.provider = 'opentelemetry' 
  AND k8s.cluster.name = 'prod-cluster' 
SINCE 1 day ago


⸻

✅ Best Practices to Control Cardinality

Practice	Description
Avoid dynamic attributes	Remove pod_uid, container_id, ip, hostname
Aggregate by stable keys	Use cluster, namespace, deployment
Use groupbyattrs	Aggregate high-frequency labels early
Normalize label values	Map variable strings (e.g., versions) into categorical labels (v1, v2)
Monitor regularly	Use New Relic → Metrics → Cardinality Analysis dashboard
Limit metric proliferation	Merge redundant custom metric names


